{Ruy Lopez: Bird Variation. Theory ends about here. From this point the game is decided by understanding rather than memory.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nd4 4. Nxd4 exd4 5. O-O c6 {Both sides have what they wanted from the opening. The middlegame will be about the centre.} 6. Bc4 Nf6 7. d3 d6 8. Nd2 ! a5 {Here the exchange is tempting and wrong: trading the good bishop leaves the dark squares bare.} (8... c5 Rb1 d5 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. a4 Be7 10. Nf3 Bg4 11. h3 Bxf3 ?! 12. Qxf3 O-O 13. Bd2 Nd7 14. Qh5 Nc5 15. f4 Nd7 16. Rae1 Nc5 17. g4 Nxa4 18. f5 h6 19. Bxh6 Bf6 20. g5 d5 21. gxf6 Qxf6 22. Bg5 g6 23. Qg4 Qg7 24. exd5 Nb6 25. fxg6 Qxg6 26. Rf6 Qg7 27. Kh1 cxd5 28. Rg1 Rfe8 29. Bf4 Qxg4 30. Rxg4+ Kf8 31. Bd6+ Re7 32. Bb5 Nd7 33. Bxd7 Rb8 34. Rh6 a4 {The endgame is winning but not simple — the passed pawn needs its king, not just its rook.} 35. Rh8# 1-0
